  "worked_solution": "The one-dimensional version of the data that preserves the most variance is the projection onto the first principal component.\n\nRecall that the principal components are the eigenvectors of the covariance matrix ${X}^{T}X.$\n\nUsing the SVD and the fact that $U$ is orthogonal (i.e., ${U}^{T}U=I$), we get that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{X}^{T}X & =(UΣ{V}^{T})^{T}(UΣ{V}^{T}) \\\\  & =V{Σ}^{T}{U}^{T}UΣ{V}^{T} \\\\  & =V{Σ}^{T}Σ{V}^{T}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $Σ$ is a diagonal matrix, and so is ${Σ}^{T}Σ,$ we know that the eigenvectors of ${X}^{T}X$ are the columns of $V.$\n\nIn particular, the first principal component is the eigenvector of ${X}^{T}X$ corresponding to the largest eigenvalue. \nAnd since $Σ$ contains the corresponding singular values (square roots of the eigenvalues) in decreasing order, the first principal component is ${v}_{1},$ the first column of $V.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Option I represents the projection of only the first datapoint (the first row of $X$) onto the first principal component. Therefore, it produces a single scalar value rather than the full one-dimensional dataset.\n\n- Option II corresponds to the one-dimensional version of the data that preserves the most variance. Indeed, it represents $X\\cdot {v}_{1},$ which is exactly the projection of the full dataset $X$ onto the first principal component.\n\n- Option III represents ${v}_{1},$ which is the first principal component. It represents a direction in feature space, not a version of the data. Notice that it is also dimensionally incorrect, since it is a $2\\times 1$ matrix instead of $4\\times 1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"",
